<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coptos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qift, Koptos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coptos, modern Qift, was an ancient Egyptian city on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. It was the starting point of desert routes through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hammamat">Wadi Hammamat</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and to stone quarries and gold mines, and the cult centre of the fertility god Min. Royal decrees of the late Old Kingdom found there record privileges granted to its temple and to powerful local officials.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Qift, ancient Gebtu and in Greek Coptos, lies in the Qena Governorate about 43 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. Its closeness to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> made it an important trading centre between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia-felix">Arabia Felix</a> and the north. It was the chief city of the fifth Upper Egyptian nome, Harawî, the Two Hawks. Mining expeditions into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-desert">Eastern Desert</a>, and expeditions to Punt, left the Nile valley from Qift and Qus. Gebtu stood at the start of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to the Red Sea, one to the port of Tjau and one further south to Berenice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> the whole trade of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> passed over these roads, and merchants followed them under the Ptolemies and in Roman and Byzantine times. Gebtu was politically important especially under the Eleventh Dynasty, but was later overshadowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. Its chief god, Min, became a male fertility deity and the god of the eastern desert, and in the Middle Kingdom he was linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> as Min-Horus.</p><p>Augustus built the city up. It fell to the Blemmyes in the third century CE and was almost destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> in 292 after a long siege. In the sixth century it was renamed Justinianopolis after the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. In 1176 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a> rose against al-Adil I, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who put the revolt down by crucifying 3,000 Copts around the city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> levelled much of the medieval town in the sixteenth century, and it never regained its former importance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The village of Qift stands on the ancient site. W. M. Flinders Petrie found remains of three temple groups inside an enclosure wall in 1893 to 1894, and American excavations took place from 1987 to 1992. The northern temple of Min and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> dates to the Ptolemaic period, on earlier foundations of the Middle Kingdom and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>. The southern temple yielded the Coptos Decrees, stelae of the Sixth and Seventh Dynasties that copy royal decrees concerning the temple and its personnel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qift” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Qakare Ibi built a small pyramid at Saqqara inscribed with Pyramid Texts, and decrees of Neferkauhor found at <b>Coptos</b> show royal favour granted to a powerful local family.</blockquote><a href="/state/eighth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
